<section class="section section--white" id="evidence" aria-labelledby="evidence-title">
  <div class="shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">Why not just use LangGraph?</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading" id="evidence-title">The same assistant, built twice, in one repository.</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">The demo repo contains one hotel-reservation assistant implemented on PicoFlow and again directly on LangGraph. Both pass the same 14-turn conversation: collect dates, search a catalog, present priced results, compare hotels, return to booking, confirm. Here is what each implementation actually contains.</p>

    <div class="artifact-compare">
      <article class="artifact artifact--pico">
        <h3>HotelFlow on PicoFlow</h3>
        <ul class="artifact__files">
          <li><code>hotel-flow.ts</code><span>51</span></li>
          <li><code>explore-step.ts</code><span>135</span></li>
          <li><code>present-step.ts</code><span>129</span></li>
          <li><code>compare-step.ts</code><span>157</span></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="artifact__total"><span>Total</span><b>472</b></p>
        <p class="artifact__note">Four domain modules. The session document, step cursor, tool dispatch, storage adapters, and concurrency checks come from the runtime.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="artifact artifact--direct">
        <h3>The same flow, direct on LangGraph</h3>
        <ul class="artifact__files">
          <li><code>hotel-langgraph.ts</code><span>1,063</span></li>
          <li><code>hotel-session-store.ts</code><span>218</span></li>
          <li><code>hotel-langgraph.state.ts</code><span>90</span></li>
          <li><code>hotel-types.ts</code><span>26</span></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="artifact__total"><span>Total</span><b>1,397</b></p>
        <p class="artifact__note">One 1,063-line graph module, plus a hand-written session store with its own memory, SQLite, and MongoDB adapters.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="artifact-takeaway">
      <p><strong>The 218-line session store is the real point.</strong> Line counts are a weak proxy, and we would not ask you to buy a runtime over them. What the counts expose is <em>which layer you end up owning</em>. A direct graph gives you topology and state primitives; the durable session, the store adapters, the revision checks, and the HTTP envelope are still yours to write and maintain — once per assistant.</p>
      <p class="artifact-takeaway__caveat">Counts are <code>wc -l</code> on the application boundary of both implementations, framework source excluded on each side, chart helpers excluded on each side. It is one honest data point from one codebase, not a benchmark. The full-project delta is smaller (2,083 vs 2,847 lines) and we publish that number too.</p>
      <div class="hero__actions">
        <a class="button button--ink" href="/compare/langgraph/">Read the full decision guide</a>
        <a class="button button--outline" href="/docs/get-started/why-picoflow/">See every measurement</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

